WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.730
Everybody loves in-jokes, and Hollywood filmmakers
are no exception.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:08.690
Tracking down their carefully concealed Easter
eggs has become a full-time hobby for some

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:12.420
fans, but even the most practiced eye occasionally
misses something.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.400
Here's a look at some well-hidden Easter eggs
that flew by even the biggest movie fans.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.529
The Departed

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:22.759
It only makes sense that a film scholar like
Martin Scorsese would pay homage to those

00:00:22.759 --> 00:00:25.570
who came before him with some well placed
Easter eggs.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:30.929
And he did just that in the 2006 film The
Departed, which used hidden X's on screen

00:00:30.929 --> 00:00:38.010
to signal which characters were going to die
- just like in the 1932 gangland classic Scarface.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.010
Even more subtle?

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:43.030
The only straight shooter in the film, Dignam,
usually appears with a straight line behind

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.030
his head.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.399
Leave it to Scorsese to hide an Easter egg
within an Easter egg!

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:48.530
Toy Story

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.329
You wouldn't expect the family friendly classic
Toy Story to be linked with the decidedly

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:57.360
non-family friendly horror opus The Shining,
but there are actually a number of Easter

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.239
eggs in the Toy Story franchise referencing
Stanley Kubrick's masterpiece.

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:02.491
The first and most subtle of them all?

00:01:02.491 --> 00:01:08.450
The carpet in Sid's house of horrors is almost
identical to the carpet in The Shining's iconic

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:09.450
hotel.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.700
We'll never look at Woody's escape quite the
same way again.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.479
Wes Craven and Sam Raimi

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:18.600
You can't really talk about the history of
horror movies over the last few decades without

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.590
referencing directors Sam Raimi and Wes Craven.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:25.829
Which is fitting, because the two also referenced
each other in nearly all of their projects.

00:01:25.829 --> 00:01:31.680
It began in Raimi's 1981 film The Evil Dead,
which included a poster of Craven's classic

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.009
The Hills Have Eyes.

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:39.259
Craven answered by having a character in 1984's
A Nightmare on Elm Street watch The Evil Dead.

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:44.159
Raimi upped the ante by placing Freddy's iconic
glove in a couple of scenes in 1987's Evil

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:49.290
Dead 2, and the friendly rivalry even continued
into the '90s with Craven's Scream showing

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.880
a group of teenagers choosing not to watch
Evil Dead.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.100
That spelled the end of their onscreen back-and-forth
until after Craven's death in 2015, when Raimi

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:03.409
paid homage in season 2 of Ash Vs. Evil Dead
by once again including a poster for The Hills

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:04.740
Have Eyes.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.500
Die Hard with a Vengeance

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:11.440
In the 1995 blockbuster Die Hard with a Vengeance,
John McClane is asked what he's been doing

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.390
since the events of Die Hard 2.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.320
He responds that he's been "smoking cigarettes
and watching Captain Kangaroo."

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.710
That's a nod to the Statler Brothers song
"Flowers on the Wall" - which his character

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:26.290
in 1994's Pulp Fiction is listening to during
a particularly memorable scene.

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:31.050
And the Easter egg had extra significance
because Die Hard with a Vengeance also featured

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.860
Pulp Fiction star Samuel L. Jackson.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:34.890
Neato!

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.610
Zootopia

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:40.550
You might not think Frozen and Zootopia have
much in common other than both being huge

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:41.550
Disney mega-hits.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.510
But midway through Zootopia, Judy and Nick
interrogate a suspect who is very familiar

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:47.510
to fans of Frozen.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.800
"What were you gonna do with those night howlers
Wessleton?"

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:50.800
"It's Weaselton!"

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:56.110
The character is voiced by Alan Tudyk, who
not coincidentally played a very similar character

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:57.110
in Frozen:

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.150
"The Duke of Weasel Town.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.150
"Wessleton!"

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.650
"The Duke of Weaselton!"

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.010
Hiring the same voice actor to set up an Easter
egg?

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.050
Now that's going above and beyond.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.560
Back to the Future

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.440
In the classic Bullwinkle segment Peabody's
Improbable History, a boy named Sherman travels

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.790
through time learning lessons from his mentor,
the super-intelligent dog Peabody.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.370
If that sounds kind of like the premise of
Back to the Future, where a boy named Marty

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.450
travels through time and learns lessons from
his mentor Doc Brown, don't worry, because

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.980
those parallels weren't lost on the folks
making Back to the Future.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:33.200
When Marty arrives in the past, he crashes
into a barn and encounters a wide-eyed kid

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.560
who thinks Marty is an alien.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:36.670
That boy's name?

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.340
Sherman Peabody.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:39.450
Well played.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:40.640
Fight Club

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:45.200
David Fincher has earned a reputation as a
meticulous director over the years, with stories

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.750
of actors having to suffer through hundreds
of takes to find the exact one he wants.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:55.040
But even for Fincher, his keen attention to
detail in Fight Club takes the cake—or the

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.950
coffee: he claims to have included a Starbucks
cup somewhere in every single scene.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:05.340
"When deep space exploration ramps up it'll
be the corporations that name everything:

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.370
Planet Starbucks."

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:12.510
There are tons of subtle hints that point
to the movie's big twist, but this might be

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:17.769
the only Easter egg that took a ridiculous
amount of planning to offer an uncanny commentary

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:22.069
on the proliferation of brand awareness and
ownership hidden in a movie that's explicitly

00:04:22.069 --> 00:04:24.949
denouncing corporate ownership of human lives.

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:25.990
Thanks for watching!

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:28.810
